{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012EbamVjhYWLpd7jwYF5QQm","type":"tool_result","content":"The page has only a few gameplay details, and most of the items you listed aren't covered.\n\n**Controls and moves**\n- The cabinet had no buttons. Players used two joysticks: \"The left joystick controlled movement while the right joystick controlled the moveset.\"\n- The article says there were \"seventeen unique moves that players could execute,\" but doesn't list them or their combinations.\n\n**Minigames and modes**\n- \"Karate Champ also featured some very interesting minigames that challenged players in between rounds.\" The page doesn't describe them.\n- A commenter (GBurke59) says a one-player version \"came out first.\"\n- Another commenter (flagg209) recalls two versions: one with fights in locations such as a back alley and a beach, and one set at a world championship. This is a recollection, not something the article confirms.\n\n**Platforms and presentation**\n- Released in U.S. arcades in 1984 by Technos Japan, published by Data East.\n- Ported to NES in 1986 and to home computers. Commenters mention the C64 and Atari 800.\n- An Arcade1Up cabinet was announced for later that year.\n\n**Not covered in the content**\n- Scoring (half and full points), the referee or judge and his speech, the timer, bonus stages (boards, vases/plants, bull), ranks or belts, stage locations beyond the commenter's recollection, the girl, and CPU behaviour are not described. The belt references in the comments concern the commenters' own martial arts training, not the game."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"2a929bdf-2eab-40b8-bc0f-c2bad0be300e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:00.698Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":58965,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page has only a few gameplay details, and most of the items you listed aren't covered.\n\n**Controls and moves**\n- The cabinet had no buttons. Players used two joysticks: \"The left joystick controlled movement while the right joystick controlled the moveset.\"\n- The article says there were \"seventeen unique moves that players could execute,\" but doesn't list them or their combinations.\n\n**Minigames and modes**\n- \"Karate Champ also featured some very interesting minigames that challenged players in between rounds.\" The page doesn't describe them.\n- A commenter (GBurke59) says a one-player version \"came out first.\"\n- Another commenter (flagg209) recalls two versions: one with fights in locations such as a back alley and a beach, and one set at a world championship. This is a recollection, not something the article confirms.\n\n**Platforms and presentation**\n- Released in U.S. arcades in 1984 by Technos Japan, published by Data East.\n- Ported to NES in 1986 and to home computers. Commenters mention the C64 and Atari 800.\n- An Arcade1Up cabinet was announced for later that year.\n\n**Not covered in the content**\n- Scoring (half and full points), the referee or judge and his speech, the timer, bonus stages (boards, vases/plants, bull), ranks or belts, stage locations beyond the commenter's recollection, the girl, and CPU behaviour are not described. The belt references in the comments concern the commenters' own martial arts training, not the game.","durationMs":7683,"url":"https://www.shacknews.com/article/109773/shacks-arcade-corner-karate-champ"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012EbamVjhYWLpd7jwYF5QQm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XfPfdMAE4pLvzsEYV6d3Nw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Karate Champ\" arcade \"belt\" rank white belt black belt \"10th dan\" judge \"half point\" \"full point\" timer 30 seconds\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ | |\",\"url\":\"https://StrategyWiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453704\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453634\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://Www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Submission #5529: £e_Nécroyeur's Arcade Karate Champ in 09:07.65\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/5529S\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://cavac.at/cavacopedia/Karate%20Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://giantbomb.com/wiki/Games/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"everygamegoing.com\",\"url\":\"https://everygamegoing.com/larticle/Karate-Champ-000/28389\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Champ\",\"url\":\"https://wikiwand.com/en/articles/Karate_Champ\"},{\"title\":\"Follow TV Tropes\",\"url\":\"https://www.tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/VideoGame/KarateChamp\"},{\"title\":\"primetimeamusements.com\",\"url\":\"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397\"},{\"title\":\"api.regvault.org\",\"url\":\"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual\"}]\n\nThe search confirmed the scoring and timer details but didn't turn up a belt progression that includes white or black belts or a 10th dan rank.\n\n- **Ranks:** Giant Bomb says players start at 1-Dan and need two matches to advance through 11 ranks, with \"Champ\" as the last. From there, players start the game at the rank of 1-Dan, and must complete two matches to proceed to the next rank (with 11 ranks total, the last being \"Champ\"). TV Tropes mentions an 8th Degree Belt level as a trigger for a computer opponent. Neither source mentions white or black belts or 10th dan.\n- **Half and full points:** StrategyWiki says a score of 500 or below gives a half point and 600 or above gives a full point. If an attack scores 500 or below, the winner earns a half point. If an attack scores 600 or above, the winner earns a full point. Primetime Amusements gives a 100 to 500 range for half points, which differs at the 500 boundary.\n- **Timer:** StrategyWiki says the clock starts at 30, and counts down once roughly every second. It pauses on a knockdown and resumes when the judge calls \"begin.\" If time runs out with neither player at two full points, the judge decides the winner (The clock pauses when one fighter is knocked down, and resumes when the judge says, \"begin.\" If the clock reaches 0 before either opponent has reach two full points, the judge chooses the winner.).\n\nThe judge's voice callouts include \"Half point\" and \"Full point,\" according to Wikipedia.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"0f7c72d5-0b81-402e-8595-b311168e87fb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:00.928Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"Karate Champ\" arcade \"belt\" rank white belt black belt \"10th dan\" judge \"half point\" \"full point\" timer 30 seconds","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FJ6Hvzhkb8CK88wpoRcMGZ","content":[{"title":"Karate Champ","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ"},{"title":"Karate Champ | |","url":"https://StrategyWiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ"},{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207"},{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou"},{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453704"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453634"},{"title":"Karate Champ","url":"https://Www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Champ"},{"title":"Submission #5529: £e_Nécroyeur's Arcade Karate Champ in 09:07.65","url":"https://tasvideos.org/5529S"},{"title":"Karate Champ","url":"https://cavac.at/cavacopedia/Karate%20Champ"},{"title":"Karate Champ","url":"https://giantbomb.com/wiki/Games/Karate_Champ"},{"title":"everygamegoing.com","url":"https://everygamegoing.com/larticle/Karate-Champ-000/28389"},{"title":"Karate Champ","url":"https://wikiwand.com/en/articles/Karate_Champ"},{"title":"Follow TV Tropes","url":"https://www.tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/VideoGame/KarateChamp"},{"title":"primetimeamusements.com","url":"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397"},{"title":"api.regvault.org","url":"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual"}]},"The search confirmed the scoring and timer details but didn't turn up a belt progression that includes white or black belts or a 10th dan rank.\n\n- **Ranks:** Giant Bomb says players start at 1-Dan and need two matches to advance through 11 ranks, with \"Champ\" as the last. From there, players start the game at the rank of 1-Dan, and must complete two matches to proceed to the next rank (with 11 ranks total, the last being \"Champ\"). TV Tropes mentions an 8th Degree Belt level as a trigger for a computer opponent. Neither source mentions white or black belts or 10th dan.\n- **Half and full points:** StrategyWiki says a score of 500 or below gives a half point and 600 or above gives a full point. If an attack scores 500 or below, the winner earns a half point. If an attack scores 600 or above, the winner earns a full point. Primetime Amusements gives a 100 to 500 range for half points, which differs at the 500 boundary.\n- **Timer:** StrategyWiki says the clock starts at 30, and counts down once roughly every second. It pauses on a knockdown and resumes when the judge calls \"begin.\" If time runs out with neither player at two full points, the judge decides the winner (The clock pauses when one fighter is knocked down, and resumes when the judge says, \"begin.\" If the clock reaches 0 before either opponent has reach two full points, the judge chooses the winner.).\n\nThe judge's voice callouts include \"Half point\" and \"Full point,\" according to Wikipedia."],"durationSeconds":7.135461322999967,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XfPfdMAE4pLvzsEYV6d3Nw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
