{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017N8Jo7vNHC4NeNNQVrsiuk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Karate Champ \"lunge punch\" \"back round kick\" \"jumping side kick\" \"front sweep\" \"reverse punch\" moves joystick\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou\"},{\"title\":\"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player\"},{\"title\":\"primetimeamusements.com\",\"url\":\"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207\"},{\"title\":\"api.regvault.org\",\"url\":\"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual\"},{\"title\":\"Karate Dou\",\"url\":\"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1261/\"},{\"title\":\"Karate%20Champ%20%28USA%29 text\",\"url\":\"https://archive.org/download/NESManuals/Karate%20Champ%20%28USA%29_text.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453634\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453704\"},{\"title\":\"Jump to content\",\"url\":\"https://forums.atariage.com/blogs/entry/16799-arcade-archives-reviews-karate-champ/\"},{\"title\":\"tasvideos.org\",\"url\":\"https://TASVideos.org/5600S\"},{\"title\":\"Controls[edit | edit source]\",\"url\":\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karateka/Gameplay\"},{\"title\":\"History of Kung Fu & Karate Games\",\"url\":\"https://lilura1.blogspot.com/2022/04/International-Karate-Plus-Atari-ST-1988-Archer-Maclean-Software-Studios.html\"},{\"title\":\"Submission #5529: £e_Nécroyeur's Arcade Karate Champ in 09:07.65\",\"url\":\"https://tasvideos.org/5529S\"},{\"title\":\"Click Here To Go To The Melbourne House Archive\",\"url\":\"https://acornelectron.co.uk/info/electron/melbourne/The-Way-Of-The-Exploding-Fist.html\"},{\"title\":\"api.regvault.org\",\"url\":\"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/e71bbc50636c6e95922f04f6d2783fd7/manual\"}]\n\nThe search turned up a joystick chart for the arcade game, with some ambiguity about the reverse punch and front sweep.\n\n- **Input layout:** Karate Champ uses two sticks, and a move comes from holding each stick in a direction. One source says that moves are performed by holding the sticks in certain directions.\n- **Arcade chart:** The Primetime Amusements chart lists the left stick down the rows and the right stick across the columns. On that chart, the lunge punch sits where both sticks point right, the back round kick where the left stick points left and the right stick points right, and the jumping side kick where the left stick points up and the right stick points right. The table is Karate Champ is a very technical game, akin to rock-papers-scissors in terms of execution.\n- **Reverse punch and front sweep:** The chart lists a reverse punch next to the front kick, but the column mapping is hard to read, so I couldn't confirm its exact input. The Karate Dou page on StrategyWiki lists both a front foot sweep and a reverse punch among its moves, but gives no joystick mapping for them. The Commodore 64 manual on RegVault uses a different layout, and it says For all moves, you must push the joystick in the direction of the desired move.\n- **Versions differ:** The NES, arcade, and C64 versions don't share the same mappings. Check the chart for the specific version you're playing.\n\nFor the exact reverse punch and front sweep inputs, the StrategyWiki and Primetime Amusements chart pages, or a version-specific manual, would be the best next step.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"4396dce6-58ca-4a94-afb5-119809ddbc83","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:27.898Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Karate Champ \"lunge punch\" \"back round kick\" \"jumping side kick\" \"front sweep\" \"reverse punch\" moves joystick","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019mbZbPsAuzAbXszg6C7RSy","content":[{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Dou"},{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Taisen_Karate_Dou"},{"title":"From StrategyWiki, the video game walkthrough and strategy guide wiki","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karate_Champ_-_Player_Vs_Player"},{"title":"primetimeamusements.com","url":"https://primetimeamusements.com/?p=22397"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207"},{"title":"api.regvault.org","url":"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/757c445d57800f0b80c12cbcb6b1ceab/manual"},{"title":"Karate Dou","url":"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1261/"},{"title":"Karate%20Champ%20%28USA%29 text","url":"https://archive.org/download/NESManuals/Karate%20Champ%20%28USA%29_text.pdf"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453634"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Posts/453704"},{"title":"Jump to content","url":"https://forums.atariage.com/blogs/entry/16799-arcade-archives-reviews-karate-champ/"},{"title":"tasvideos.org","url":"https://TASVideos.org/5600S"},{"title":"Controls[edit | edit source]","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Karateka/Gameplay"},{"title":"History of Kung Fu & Karate Games","url":"https://lilura1.blogspot.com/2022/04/International-Karate-Plus-Atari-ST-1988-Archer-Maclean-Software-Studios.html"},{"title":"Submission #5529: £e_Nécroyeur's Arcade Karate Champ in 09:07.65","url":"https://tasvideos.org/5529S"},{"title":"Click Here To Go To The Melbourne House Archive","url":"https://acornelectron.co.uk/info/electron/melbourne/The-Way-Of-The-Exploding-Fist.html"},{"title":"api.regvault.org","url":"https://api.regvault.org/api/v1/game/c64/e71bbc50636c6e95922f04f6d2783fd7/manual"}]},"The search turned up a joystick chart for the arcade game, with some ambiguity about the reverse punch and front sweep.\n\n- **Input layout:** Karate Champ uses two sticks, and a move comes from holding each stick in a direction. One source says that moves are performed by holding the sticks in certain directions.\n- **Arcade chart:** The Primetime Amusements chart lists the left stick down the rows and the right stick across the columns. On that chart, the lunge punch sits where both sticks point right, the back round kick where the left stick points left and the right stick points right, and the jumping side kick where the left stick points up and the right stick points right. The table is Karate Champ is a very technical game, akin to rock-papers-scissors in terms of execution.\n- **Reverse punch and front sweep:** The chart lists a reverse punch next to the front kick, but the column mapping is hard to read, so I couldn't confirm its exact input. The Karate Dou page on StrategyWiki lists both a front foot sweep and a reverse punch among its moves, but gives no joystick mapping for them. The Commodore 64 manual on RegVault uses a different layout, and it says For all moves, you must push the joystick in the direction of the desired move.\n- **Versions differ:** The NES, arcade, and C64 versions don't share the same mappings. Check the chart for the specific version you're playing.\n\nFor the exact reverse punch and front sweep inputs, the StrategyWiki and Primetime Amusements chart pages, or a version-specific manual, would be the best next step."],"durationSeconds":9.832934232000028,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017N8Jo7vNHC4NeNNQVrsiuk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EdwsRPHWrw7SMMJd7HWTYq","type":"tool_result","content":"**Karate Champ (Data East arcade, 1984) mechanics, per the submission page**\n\n**Controls**\n- The control scheme uses \"2 4-way joysticks and no buttons.\" The left stick controls movement and posture, and the right stick controls attacks.\n- Combining the two sticks gives about 24 controls.\n\n**Techniques (total frames, points, scoring)**\n- **Front Kick:** 54 frames, 100-200 pts, half point.\n- **Middle Reverse Punch:** 34 frames, 100-200 pts, half point. Only occurs within 62 px of the Opponent.\n- **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch:** 57 frames, 400-800 pts, half or full point.\n- **Jumping Side Kick:** 62 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point.\n- **Low Kick:** 52 frames, 100-200 pts, half point. Uses a 4-frame posture delay.\n- **Round Kick:** 44 frames, 300-600 pts, half or full point. The fastest technique able to score a full point.\n- **Back Round Kick:** 51 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point.\n- **Turn Back Kick:** 57 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Turns the Hero to face the Opponent first (5 frames).\n- **Front Foot Sweep:** 78 frames, 200-400 pts, half point.\n- **Back Foot Sweep:** 75 frames, 100-200 pts, half point.\n- **Back Kick:** 49 frames, 200-400 pts, half point.\n- **Middle Lunge Punch:** 55 frames, 200-400 pts, half point. Cannot occur within 62 px; input becomes a Front Kick.\n- **Upper Punch:** 53 frames, 300-600 pts, half or full point. Left stick is only needed on the first input frame.\n- **Jumping Back Kick:** 52 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Left stick Up, right stick Left, held up to 25 frames. Turns the Hero around.\n- **Upper Lunge Punch:** 55 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Cannot occur within 62 px (becomes a Round Kick). Reaches about 75 px.\n- **Block:** 1 frame to start, 22 frames overall. Low, middle, or high, set by the Opponent's technique. Cannot block Low Kick, Front Foot Sweep, or Back Foot Sweep.\n- **Crouch:** 30 frames. **Jump:** 47 frames. **Forward and Backward Somersault:** 70 frames each.\n\n**Scoring**\n- Each Technique's point value is a range, and the page notes half or full points as a tag on each move.\n- If the Opponent is mid-Technique when the Hero's Technique lands, the Hero generally scores double points. If the Technique is capable, this yields a full point.\n- Each remaining second of Time when the Opponent is defeated scores 100 points.\n- Practice awards 200 points per correctly matched senpai Technique.\n- Dojo Matches have no Time bonus.\n\n**Judge and timer**\n- The TASVideos judge for this submission is feos, and the publisher is fsvgm777. The page does not describe the game's own judging beyond the transition timing.\n- The Technique score appears after the hit animation ends. The Technique Score Display Timer is set to 90 and the Transition Timer to 40. Judgment is rendered when the Transition Timer expires.\n- Time stops decrementing when the Opponent is hit.\n- One second of Time expires on the first Round frame, then one second per 60 frames.\n\n**CPU (Opponent) behaviour**\n- A 1-frame delay can change the Opponent's action, if used while the Hero is Ready and the Posture Delay Timer is inactive.\n- Starting Front Foot Sweep on the first possible frame always makes the Opponent Jump.\n- In later Matches, an early Low Kick makes the Opponent immediately use Jumping Side Kick.\n- From 9th Dan, the Opponent's posture delay drops by 1 frame per posture. At Champ, it drops by 2 frames. The page describes the 9th Dan CPU as able to block, evade, and counterattack, with specific reactions based on the Hero's attack height.\n\n**Bonus stages**\n- **Evade (6 intervals: Bonus Intervals 1, 3, 7, 11, 15, 19):** Objects such as bonsai and boulders are thrown. Each destroyed object is worth 200 pts. Evade ends when all objects are destroyed or the Hero is hit.\n- **Trial Chop (5 intervals: Bonus Intervals 2, 5, 9, 13, 17):** The page says pressing on the earliest possible frame saves 58 frames compared to getting Perfect.\n- **Get The Bull (9 intervals: Bonus Intervals 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20):** Hitting the Bull is worth 3,000 pts. Upper Punch, Upper Lunge Punch, Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch, Front Kick, and Back Kick can hit it. The Bull cannot be evaded. Being hit by the Bull on the earliest frame is 43 frames faster than hitting it on the earliest frame.\n- **Boards and vases:** The page does not describe these as gameplay stages. It says the kicking apple, board, and vase scenes appear only in attract mode.\n\n**Ranks and stage count**\n- Ranks run from Beginner through 1st to 10th Dan, then Champ.\n- There are 21 Matches: 2 Dojo Matches and 19 National Championship Matches. Each Match has 2 Rounds.\n- The page says defeating the 20th Opponent grants Champ, which is above 10th Dan.\n- The page states that the Champ rank is reached before the most difficult Opponent.\n\n**Tricks and exploits**\n- **Rapid Advance:** Press Up on the right stick for 1 frame, delay 2 frames, and repeat. This moves the Hero 2 px per frame. Round Kick is the only move that can be cancelled this way, because it does not immediately set the Posture Delay Timer.\n- **Opponent manipulation:** Frame delays and partially started Techniques change the Opponent's responses.\n- **Behind-the-Opponent placement:** Certain Techniques at close range can put the Hero behind the Opponent.\n- **Lag:** The game occasionally lags for 1 frame, especially during Get The Bull.\n- **Run details:** Arcade, MAME-rr 0.139, Hard difficulty, 32,859 frames at 60 fps, 09:07.65, optimized for TAS Timing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"43fe0926-8f8c-4348-b54f-ec74b5605a9d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:40.352Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":41824,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Karate Champ (Data East arcade, 1984) mechanics, per the submission page**\n\n**Controls**\n- The control scheme uses \"2 4-way joysticks and no buttons.\" The left stick controls movement and posture, and the right stick controls attacks.\n- Combining the two sticks gives about 24 controls.\n\n**Techniques (total frames, points, scoring)**\n- **Front Kick:** 54 frames, 100-200 pts, half point.\n- **Middle Reverse Punch:** 34 frames, 100-200 pts, half point. Only occurs within 62 px of the Opponent.\n- **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch:** 57 frames, 400-800 pts, half or full point.\n- **Jumping Side Kick:** 62 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point.\n- **Low Kick:** 52 frames, 100-200 pts, half point. Uses a 4-frame posture delay.\n- **Round Kick:** 44 frames, 300-600 pts, half or full point. The fastest technique able to score a full point.\n- **Back Round Kick:** 51 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point.\n- **Turn Back Kick:** 57 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Turns the Hero to face the Opponent first (5 frames).\n- **Front Foot Sweep:** 78 frames, 200-400 pts, half point.\n- **Back Foot Sweep:** 75 frames, 100-200 pts, half point.\n- **Back Kick:** 49 frames, 200-400 pts, half point.\n- **Middle Lunge Punch:** 55 frames, 200-400 pts, half point. Cannot occur within 62 px; input becomes a Front Kick.\n- **Upper Punch:** 53 frames, 300-600 pts, half or full point. Left stick is only needed on the first input frame.\n- **Jumping Back Kick:** 52 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Left stick Up, right stick Left, held up to 25 frames. Turns the Hero around.\n- **Upper Lunge Punch:** 55 frames, 500-1000 pts, half or full point. Cannot occur within 62 px (becomes a Round Kick). Reaches about 75 px.\n- **Block:** 1 frame to start, 22 frames overall. Low, middle, or high, set by the Opponent's technique. Cannot block Low Kick, Front Foot Sweep, or Back Foot Sweep.\n- **Crouch:** 30 frames. **Jump:** 47 frames. **Forward and Backward Somersault:** 70 frames each.\n\n**Scoring**\n- Each Technique's point value is a range, and the page notes half or full points as a tag on each move.\n- If the Opponent is mid-Technique when the Hero's Technique lands, the Hero generally scores double points. If the Technique is capable, this yields a full point.\n- Each remaining second of Time when the Opponent is defeated scores 100 points.\n- Practice awards 200 points per correctly matched senpai Technique.\n- Dojo Matches have no Time bonus.\n\n**Judge and timer**\n- The TASVideos judge for this submission is feos, and the publisher is fsvgm777. The page does not describe the game's own judging beyond the transition timing.\n- The Technique score appears after the hit animation ends. The Technique Score Display Timer is set to 90 and the Transition Timer to 40. Judgment is rendered when the Transition Timer expires.\n- Time stops decrementing when the Opponent is hit.\n- One second of Time expires on the first Round frame, then one second per 60 frames.\n\n**CPU (Opponent) behaviour**\n- A 1-frame delay can change the Opponent's action, if used while the Hero is Ready and the Posture Delay Timer is inactive.\n- Starting Front Foot Sweep on the first possible frame always makes the Opponent Jump.\n- In later Matches, an early Low Kick makes the Opponent immediately use Jumping Side Kick.\n- From 9th Dan, the Opponent's posture delay drops by 1 frame per posture. At Champ, it drops by 2 frames. The page describes the 9th Dan CPU as able to block, evade, and counterattack, with specific reactions based on the Hero's attack height.\n\n**Bonus stages**\n- **Evade (6 intervals: Bonus Intervals 1, 3, 7, 11, 15, 19):** Objects such as bonsai and boulders are thrown. Each destroyed object is worth 200 pts. Evade ends when all objects are destroyed or the Hero is hit.\n- **Trial Chop (5 intervals: Bonus Intervals 2, 5, 9, 13, 17):** The page says pressing on the earliest possible frame saves 58 frames compared to getting Perfect.\n- **Get The Bull (9 intervals: Bonus Intervals 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20):** Hitting the Bull is worth 3,000 pts. Upper Punch, Upper Lunge Punch, Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch, Front Kick, and Back Kick can hit it. The Bull cannot be evaded. Being hit by the Bull on the earliest frame is 43 frames faster than hitting it on the earliest frame.\n- **Boards and vases:** The page does not describe these as gameplay stages. It says the kicking apple, board, and vase scenes appear only in attract mode.\n\n**Ranks and stage count**\n- Ranks run from Beginner through 1st to 10th Dan, then Champ.\n- There are 21 Matches: 2 Dojo Matches and 19 National Championship Matches. Each Match has 2 Rounds.\n- The page says defeating the 20th Opponent grants Champ, which is above 10th Dan.\n- The page states that the Champ rank is reached before the most difficult Opponent.\n\n**Tricks and exploits**\n- **Rapid Advance:** Press Up on the right stick for 1 frame, delay 2 frames, and repeat. This moves the Hero 2 px per frame. Round Kick is the only move that can be cancelled this way, because it does not immediately set the Posture Delay Timer.\n- **Opponent manipulation:** Frame delays and partially started Techniques change the Opponent's responses.\n- **Behind-the-Opponent placement:** Certain Techniques at close range can put the Hero behind the Opponent.\n- **Lag:** The game occasionally lags for 1 frame, especially during Get The Bull.\n- **Run details:** Arcade, MAME-rr 0.139, Hard difficulty, 32,859 frames at 60 fps, 09:07.65, optimized for TAS Timing.","durationMs":22923,"url":"https://tasvideos.org/5529S"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EdwsRPHWrw7SMMJd7HWTYq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
