{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0191BRmj1G8ooN46M9H632gD","type":"tool_result","content":"The page doesn't contain most of the items you listed. Here's what it does include:\n\n**Controls:** The page doesn't chart specific stick-to-technique mappings. It says the game used \"Double 4-way joysticks\" and that \"simultaneous joystick manipulation required to execute even the simplest of kicks.\"\n\n**Bonus stages:** \"After every second victory, the player has a chance to earn bonus points by dodging or striking targets.\"\n\n**Not on the page:** judge trivia, speech lines, the girl, stage names, ranks, and designer/staff credits.\n\n**Release info:**\n- \"Karate Dou was released in September 1984 in Japan.\"\n- \"This game is known outside Japan as Karate Champ.\"\n- Copyright: \"Karate Dou © 1984 Data East.\"\n- Ports: Famicom Disk (July 22, 1988, as Karate Champ), PlayStation 2 (July 21, 2005, as *Karate Dou [Oretachi Geasen Zoku]*), PS4 PSN (October 9, 2014, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*), and PS4 PSN (July 2, 2015, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*, listed as an [AS] entry)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"c3d9b652-18ad-4619-9129-9c071139f5a6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:56:56.294Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":47626,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page doesn't contain most of the items you listed. Here's what it does include:\n\n**Controls:** The page doesn't chart specific stick-to-technique mappings. It says the game used \"Double 4-way joysticks\" and that \"simultaneous joystick manipulation required to execute even the simplest of kicks.\"\n\n**Bonus stages:** \"After every second victory, the player has a chance to earn bonus points by dodging or striking targets.\"\n\n**Not on the page:** judge trivia, speech lines, the girl, stage names, ranks, and designer/staff credits.\n\n**Release info:**\n- \"Karate Dou was released in September 1984 in Japan.\"\n- \"This game is known outside Japan as Karate Champ.\"\n- Copyright: \"Karate Dou © 1984 Data East.\"\n- Ports: Famicom Disk (July 22, 1988, as Karate Champ), PlayStation 2 (July 21, 2005, as *Karate Dou [Oretachi Geasen Zoku]*), PS4 PSN (October 9, 2014, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*), and PS4 PSN (July 2, 2015, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*, listed as an [AS] entry).","durationMs":5950,"url":"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1261/"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0191BRmj1G8ooN46M9H632gD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUTNDkwQ7Wv7DS1mNY3JUc","type":"tool_result","content":"The page has no complete input chart. It describes techniques, frame counts, and scoring, but gives input mappings only for some moves, so I've included what's there.\n\n**Technique scoring and frames** (frames to execute / overall frames; points; scoring type)\n\n| Technique | Overall frames | Points | Scores |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Front Kick | 54 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Middle Reverse Punch | 34 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | 57 | 400–800 | Half or Full |\n| Jumping Side Kick | 62 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Low Kick | 52 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Round Kick | 44 | 300–600 | Half or Full |\n| Back Round Kick | 51 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Turn Back Kick | 57 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Front Foot Sweep | 78 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Back Foot Sweep | 75 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Back Kick | 49 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Middle Lunge Punch | 55 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Upper Punch | 53 | 300–600 | Half or Full |\n| Jumping Back Kick | 52 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Upper Lunge Punch | 55 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n\nRound Kick is the fastest technique that can score a Full Point.\n\n**Input notes**\n- Rapid Advance: Up on the Right Joystick for 1 frame.\n- Jumping Back Kick: Left Joystick Up plus Right Joystick Left, held for up to 25 frames.\n- Upper Punch: the Left Joystick is needed only on the first input frame.\n- Middle Reverse Punch becomes Front Kick if the Left Joystick is held Right while the Right Joystick is held Right.\n- Middle Lunge Punch and Upper Lunge Punch within 62 pixels of the opponent become Front Kick and Round Kick.\n- Blocks don't stop Low Kick, Front Foot Sweep, or Back Foot Sweep.\n\n**Judging and scoring**\n- A Technique that hits while the opponent is mid-attack generally scores double points, and a capable Technique scores a Full Point.\n- Judgment happens after the Transition Timer expires, so the goal is the Technique needing the fewest total frames, not just the fastest hit.\n- Each second of Time left when the opponent is defeated scores 100 points. Time stops when the opponent is hit.\n- Dojo Matches have no Time bonus.\n\n**Bonus stages**\n- Evade: 200 points per object destroyed.\n- Get The Bull: hitting the Bull is worth 3,000 points.\n- Trial Chop: pressing on the earliest frame saves 58 frames compared to getting Perfect. The page doesn't give point values.\n\n**Practice (senpai)**\n- Matching a demonstrated Technique within 50 frames awards 200 points.\n- Matching on the earliest possible frame is fastest.\n\n**CPU AI**\n- Delaying by as little as 1 frame can change the opponent's action.\n- Front Foot Sweep on the first possible frame always makes the opponent Jump.\n- At 9th and 10th Dan, the opponent's posture delay drops by 1 frame per posture. At Champ it drops by 2.\n\n**Ranks and stages**\n- A Stage is 2 Matches. Ranks display the current Stage through Stage 10, then show \"CMP\" (Champ) from Stage 11.\n- Defeating the 20th opponent grants the Karate Champ rank.\n- The page doesn't say whether the game ever ends. A forum post says this run completes one Stage (Stage 11) at maximum difficulty."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"37137cfb-c212-4555-b65d-e61e685b1e5a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:57:05.765Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":93098,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page has no complete input chart. It describes techniques, frame counts, and scoring, but gives input mappings only for some moves, so I've included what's there.\n\n**Technique scoring and frames** (frames to execute / overall frames; points; scoring type)\n\n| Technique | Overall frames | Points | Scores |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Front Kick | 54 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Middle Reverse Punch | 34 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | 57 | 400–800 | Half or Full |\n| Jumping Side Kick | 62 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Low Kick | 52 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Round Kick | 44 | 300–600 | Half or Full |\n| Back Round Kick | 51 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Turn Back Kick | 57 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Front Foot Sweep | 78 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Back Foot Sweep | 75 | 100–200 | Half |\n| Back Kick | 49 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Middle Lunge Punch | 55 | 200–400 | Half |\n| Upper Punch | 53 | 300–600 | Half or Full |\n| Jumping Back Kick | 52 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n| Upper Lunge Punch | 55 | 500–1000 | Half or Full |\n\nRound Kick is the fastest technique that can score a Full Point.\n\n**Input notes**\n- Rapid Advance: Up on the Right Joystick for 1 frame.\n- Jumping Back Kick: Left Joystick Up plus Right Joystick Left, held for up to 25 frames.\n- Upper Punch: the Left Joystick is needed only on the first input frame.\n- Middle Reverse Punch becomes Front Kick if the Left Joystick is held Right while the Right Joystick is held Right.\n- Middle Lunge Punch and Upper Lunge Punch within 62 pixels of the opponent become Front Kick and Round Kick.\n- Blocks don't stop Low Kick, Front Foot Sweep, or Back Foot Sweep.\n\n**Judging and scoring**\n- A Technique that hits while the opponent is mid-attack generally scores double points, and a capable Technique scores a Full Point.\n- Judgment happens after the Transition Timer expires, so the goal is the Technique needing the fewest total frames, not just the fastest hit.\n- Each second of Time left when the opponent is defeated scores 100 points. Time stops when the opponent is hit.\n- Dojo Matches have no Time bonus.\n\n**Bonus stages**\n- Evade: 200 points per object destroyed.\n- Get The Bull: hitting the Bull is worth 3,000 points.\n- Trial Chop: pressing on the earliest frame saves 58 frames compared to getting Perfect. The page doesn't give point values.\n\n**Practice (senpai)**\n- Matching a demonstrated Technique within 50 frames awards 200 points.\n- Matching on the earliest possible frame is fastest.\n\n**CPU AI**\n- Delaying by as little as 1 frame can change the opponent's action.\n- Front Foot Sweep on the first possible frame always makes the opponent Jump.\n- At 9th and 10th Dan, the opponent's posture delay drops by 1 frame per posture. At Champ it drops by 2.\n\n**Ranks and stages**\n- A Stage is 2 Matches. Ranks display the current Stage through Stage 10, then show \"CMP\" (Champ) from Stage 11.\n- Defeating the 20th opponent grants the Karate Champ rank.\n- The page doesn't say whether the game ever ends. A forum post says this run completes one Stage (Stage 11) at maximum difficulty.","durationMs":13793,"url":"https://tasvideos.org/Forum/Topics/19207"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RUTNDkwQ7Wv7DS1mNY3JUc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
