{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2PL3CnXfresrdt56Um","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017XFS1pqFusxek1EXMRL4WM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174743__x8534bd/work/PLAN.md","offset":76,"limit":160},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3551,"cache_read_input_tokens":433805,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3551},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"cecb700f-71dc-400a-a7c6-6b600de79702","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:31:51.150Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2PKopZTpCVyiZPBbcD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017XFS1pqFusxek1EXMRL4WM":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174743__x8534bd/work/PLAN.md","offset":76,"limit":160}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017XFS1pqFusxek1EXMRL4WM","type":"tool_result","content":"76\t## 3. Controls and UX\n77\t\n78\t### 3.1 The twin-stick technique system (the heart of the game)\n79\t- **Left stick = posture** (where your body goes): Neutral, Toward the foe, Away, Up (jump), Down (crouch).\n80\t- **Right stick = technique** (where your strike goes): ↑ high, → (toward the foe) middle, ↓ low, ← (away) the *spinning* family.\n81\t- Directions are **relative to the foe**: \"toward\" is the side the opponent is on, not the screen's right. Fighters face their foe automatically whenever they are free and on the ground.\n82\t- A technique starts on the **press edge** of the technique stick (neutral → a direction), combined with the posture held at that moment. Holding the technique stick does not repeat; it must return to neutral.\n83\t- **Late-posture upgrade window:** if the posture changes from Neutral to a direction within **4 frames (8 steps at 120 Hz)** after a standing technique starts, swap to that posture's variant and keep the elapsed startup frames. This makes \"both sticks at once\" forgiving and adds no latency.\n84\t- **Buffer:** a technique press made while busy (recovery, blockstun) is kept for **0.15 s** (`input.buffered`) and fires on the first free frame.\n85\t- **4-way gates:** both sticks are quantized to 4 directions. Analog sticks use the dominant axis, press at 0.5 and release below 0.35 (hysteresis). For digital posture, vertical beats horizontal (W+D = Up). For the technique keys, the most recent press wins; if two are truly simultaneous, the order is ↑ > ↓ > horizontal.\n86\t\n87\t**The technique matrix** (L posture × R technique). The 15 scoring techniques are in **bold**.\n88\t\n89\t| L posture \\ R technique | R ↑ (high) | R → (toward) | R ↓ (low) | R ← (away / spin) |\n90\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n91\t| **Neutral** (stand) | **Round Kick** | **Front Kick** (close: **Reverse Punch**) | **Low Kick** | **Back Kick** |\n92\t| **Toward** (step in) | **Upper Lunge Punch** (too close: Round Kick) | **Middle Lunge Punch** (too close: Front Kick) | **Front Foot Sweep** | **Turn Back Kick** |\n93\t| **Away** (step back) | **Back Round Kick** | Block (auto height) | **Back Foot Sweep** | Back Somersault |\n94\t| **Up** (jump) | Front Somersault (flips over the foe) | **Jumping Side Kick** | Jump (straight) | **Jumping Back Kick** |\n95\t| **Down** (crouch) | **Upper Punch** (anti-air) | **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch** | Crouch | Back Roll |\n96\t\n97\tPosture alone: Neutral = kamae idle, Toward = walk in (72 u/s), Away = walk back (56 u/s), Up = straight jump, Down = crouch while held. With walking counted, that makes about two dozen moves, matching the source's \"24\".\n98\t\n99\t### 3.2 Device mappings\n100\tEngine action names are given in `code`. The game defines its own input preset, `KC_KEYS` (§15.4 EC1 adds the right-stick codes).\n101\t\n102\t| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping, DualSense first) | Keyboard (1 player) | Mouse |\n103\t|---|---|---|---|\n104\t| Posture `up/down/left/right` | Left stick, D-pad | W A S D (arrows also work; the engine fixes them as moves) | — |\n105\t| Technique `tUp/tDown/tLeft/tRight` | **Right stick** (`PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, EC1) **or face buttons**: △ (Pad3) = tUp, ✕ (Pad0) = tDown, □ (Pad2) = tLeft, ○ (Pad1) = tRight | **I K J L** (plus Numpad 8 5/2 4 6) | — |\n106\t| `pause` | Options (Pad9); ignored while Create (Pad8) is held, which is the AI Hub's chord | Esc, P | — |\n107\t| `start` | Options | Enter | — |\n108\t| Menus `confirm` / `cancel` | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right-click; hover moves the focus; the wheel scrolls lists |\n109\t| Dev panel hotkey (only after dev mode is enabled once) | L3+R3 | F2 or Backquote | — |\n110\t\n111\t- **Local 2 players on one keyboard** (`game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`, then `input.use()` with explicit maps; do not rely on `halfKeys`' automatic order): **P1** posture W A S D, technique **T F G H** (T up, G down, F left, H right). **P2** posture arrows, technique **I J K L** and Numpad 8 4 5 6 (5 or 2 = down).\n112\t- Menus accept every joined player's confirm and cancel (the engine's `E.Menu` reads `game._players`).\n113\t- **Face buttons double as a technique stick.** ✕ = confirm in menus, but in a fight there is no confirm; skip prompts use `start` only.\n114\t\n115\t### 3.3 Prompts and glyphs\n116\t- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On a gamepad they show **PlayStation glyphs by default**. Set the engine's standard setting `glyphs` (Controls → Controller → *Button symbols*) to default to `'playstation'` (EC2); players can switch it to **Xbox** (A B X Y). The keyboard shows keycaps and the mouse shows mouse icons.\n117\t- **The 5×7 pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △** (verified: it holds ASCII plus ♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • © × ▸ ♪). Build an icon renderer, `KC.glyph(g, x, y, code, size)`, that draws:\n118\t  - PS badges: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ad8a0`, each on a dark round badge.\n119\t  - Xbox badges: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow.\n120\t  - Stick icons: L or R with a direction arrow, for twin-stick prompts.\n121\t  - Keycaps: a rounded light rectangle with the key label; Space is wide.\n122\t  - Mouse icons with the button highlighted.\n123\t  - Also add ✕ ○ □ △ glyphs to the font (EC3) as a fallback.\n124\t- A technique prompt is two icons, `[L→] + [R↑]` on a pad and `[D] + [I]` on a keyboard. Build it with `KC.promptFor(techId, device)`.\n125\t\n126\t### 3.4 10-foot rules (all screens)\n127\t- Primary menu text uses `scale: 2`. Use no more than 7 items per menu and one primary action per screen. The focus highlight is big and animated (a pulsing bar plus a ▸ cursor). Hold to repeat.\n128\t- A bottom **prompt bar** always shows confirm and back (for example \"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK\", drawn with glyph icons).\n129\t- Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates. The pixel menus use `E.Menu`, which already does this; custom grids (select, stage, records) must implement hover and click with `input.pointer()` and `input.clicked()`.\n130\t- Avoid dense data screens. The records screen shows 10 big rows. The move list is interactive, one posture row at a time (§8.6). The About screen is paged, at most 8 lines per page.\n131\t- Keep titles and pauses clear of the engine's top-right corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`, about 110×60 CSS px).\n132\t\n133\t### 3.5 Touch-only devices\n134\t- Detection: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet. Re-check on every `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`; any keyboard or pad input lifts the gate at once.\n135\t- Set `input.touchEnabled = false` and give every scene `touch: []`, so no touch buttons or stick ever show.\n136\t- The title shows a centred panel: **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, with the engine's corner **settings/menu button** still visible (it opens the standard settings). The attract mode still starts after 10 s and loops. Taps on the canvas only return to the title panel, where the 10 s idle timer restarts the demo.\n137\t\n138\t---\n139\t\n140\t## 4. Combat system\n141\t\n142\t### 4.1 Fighter body and stage geometry\n143\t- The arena walls are at `x0 = 0` and `x1 = 400`. The marks are at **x = 150 (white, left)** and **x = 250 (red, right)**. `y = 0` is the fight plane and `z` is up (side view).\n144\t- Rigs are `new E.Humanoid({ size: 2.2, build: 'heroic', weapon: null, … })`, about 72 px tall at 240 px. The body is an `E.Platformer` on an `E.FightStage(0, 400)` (from the fight module): `w = 8 × size ≈ 18`, `h = 30 × size = 66`. Use the same constructor numbers as `E.Fighter` (`accel 2400, decel 3200, airAccel 0, coyote 0, buffer 0, cut 1`).\n145\t- Movement: walk toward 72 u/s and away 56 u/s. Jump `vz 360` with gravity 1100 (apex ≈ 59 u, ≈ 0.65 s or 39 frames in the air). Front somersault `vz 370`, `vx +140` toward the foe (40 frames in the air, 94 u of travel; it crosses a foe within a gap of ≈ 70). Back somersault `vz 370, vx −126` (85 u). Back roll travels 90 u away in 0.47 s along the ground.\n146\t- **Per-fighter stats and tuning multiply the table:** walk speed × `stats.walk`; reach × `stats.reach` × `TUNE.reachScale`; technique frames ÷ (`stats.speed` × `TUNE.techSpeed`), rounded to whole 120 Hz steps.\n147\t- Push boxes never overlap. Resolve them as `E.Fighter.exchange` does: shared correction, with walls respected.\n148\t- **Hurt boxes come from rig joints** (copy `E.Fighter._boxes()`): head, body and legs. **Hit box:** during active frames, a circle of radius `hitR × size / 2` at the striking joint (`footR`, `handR`, `handL` or `heel`). Crouching lowers the head and body boxes; jumps and flips raise the legs. The **explicit height rules (§4.3) are applied as well**, so results never depend on animation alone.\n149\t\n150\t### 4.2 Technique table (authoritative data; frames are at 60 fps, and the engine steps at 120 Hz, so multiply by 2)\n151\t\"Gap\" means the distance between the two push-box edges at contact. Points go in steps of 100. \"Full\" means the technique can reach a full point (600 or more) without a counter. The source total is the TASVideos figure; ours is about 85% of it, for a slightly snappier modern feel.\n152\t\n153\t| id | Name (JP) | Input | Height | Startup / active / recovery | Total (source) | Travel | Reach (gap) | Points | Full |\n154\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n155\t| `frontKick` | Front Kick (mae-geri) | N + → | mid | 11 / 5 / 28 | 44 (54) | 0 | 6–30 | 100–200 | no |\n156\t| `revPunch` | Reverse Punch (gyaku-zuki) | N + → when gap < 12 | mid | 6 / 4 / 18 | 28 (34) | +4 | 0–14 | 100–200 | no |\n157\t| `roundKick` | Round Kick (mawashi-geri) | N + ↑ | high | 10 / 5 / 22 | 37 (44) | 0 | 6–30 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n158\t| `lowKick` | Low Kick (gedan-geri) | N + ↓ | low, unblockable | 10 / 5 / 28 | 43 (52) | 0 | 4–28 | 100–200 | no |\n159\t| `backKick` | Back Kick (ushiro-geri) | N + ← | mid | 13 / 5 / 23 | 41 (49) | +6 | 10–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n160\t| `upLunge` | Upper Lunge Punch (jōdan oi-zuki) | → + ↑ (gap < 18: `roundKick`) | high | 15 / 5 / 26 | 46 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 500–1000 | yes |\n161\t| `midLunge` | Middle Lunge Punch (chūdan oi-zuki) | → + → (gap < 18: `frontKick`) | mid | 14 / 5 / 26 | 45 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 200–400 | counter only |\n162\t| `frontSweep` | Front Foot Sweep (ashi-barai) | → + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (78) | +10 | 4–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n163\t| `turnBackKick` | Turn Back Kick | → + ← | mid | 17 / 5 / 26 | 48 (57) | +12 | 12–40 | 500–1000 | yes |\n164\t| `backRound` | Back Round Kick (ushiro-mawashi-geri) | ← + ↑ | high | 15 / 5 / 23 | 43 (51) | 0 | 8–34 | 500–1000 | yes |\n165\t| `backSweep` | Back Foot Sweep (spinning sweep) | ← + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (75) | 0 | 6–34 | 100–200 | no |\n166\t| `jumpSide` | Jumping Side Kick (tobi yoko-geri) | ↑ + → | high (air) | 16 / 10 / 26 | 52 (62) | +60 in the air | 30–90 | 500–1000 | yes |\n167\t| `jumpBack` | Jumping Back Kick | ↑ + ← | high (air) | 14 / 8 / 22 | 44 (52) | +20 in the air | 8–48 | 500–1000 | yes |\n168\t| `upperPunch` | Upper Punch (age-zuki) | ↓ + ↑ | high, rising (also hits airborne foes in the first half of their arc) | 8 / 7 / 30 | 45 (53) | 0 | 0–18 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n169\t| `squatPunch` | Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | ↓ + → | mid-low (also hits crouchers) | 13 / 5 / 30 | 48 (57) | +20 | 10–44 | 400–800 | yes |\n170\t| `block` | Block (uke) | ← + → | auto high or mid | 1 / 14 guard / 4 | 19 (22) | 0 | — | — | — |\n171\t| `frontFlip` | Front Somersault | ↑ + ↑ | evasive | 6 crouch, 40 air, 12 land | 58 (70) | +95 | — | — | — |\n172\t| `backFlip` | Back Somersault | ← + ← | evasive | 6 / 40 / 12 | 58 (70) | −85 | — | — | — |\n173\t| `jump` | Jump | ↑ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 4 / 39 air / 5 | 48 (47) | 0 | — | — | — |\n174\t| `crouch` | Crouch | ↓ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 3 in, hold, 5 out | — | — | — | — | — |\n175\t| `backRoll` | Back Roll | ↓ + ← | evasive (low profile, hurt height 24) | 4 / 28 / 8 | 40 (—) | −90 | — | — | — |\n176\t\n177\t**Close substitutions chain:** a Middle Lunge Punch at gap < 12 becomes a Front Kick, and then a Reverse Punch.\n178\t\n179\tStore this as `KC.DATA.techniques` (schema in §15.3). Each entry also carries:\n180\t- `move`: the rig move spec (§10.2);\n181\t- `limb` and `hitR`;\n182\t- `kiai`: `'light'` or `'heavy'`;\n183\t- `bull: true` for the five techniques that work on the bull (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`, as in the source);\n184\t- `potHeights`: the bonus-stage heights the technique can break.\n185\t\n186\t**The Technique Lab (§14) must measure each technique's real contact gap against a standing dummy, and the tests assert the table within ±4 u.** Tune the rig `reach`/`r1`/`lunge` values until it matches; never change the table to fit the animation.\n187\t\n188\t### 4.3 Height and state rules\n189\t- **High** techniques whiff against a **crouching** foe or one in a **back roll**.\n190\t- **Low** techniques whiff against an **airborne** foe (jump, flips, jump kicks).\n191\t- **Mid** techniques hit standing foes. `squatPunch` also hits crouching foes.\n192\t- `upperPunch` hits standing foes (the head) and **airborne foes up to z 70**. Against an airborne foe it gets +0.25 quality (the anti-air reward).\n193\t- **Somersaults:** while airborne, the tucked body can be hit only by high techniques and `upperPunch`; mid and low pass under it.\n194\t- A fighter that is **down**, **rising**, **frozen** or **returning** cannot be hit.\n195\t\n196\t### 4.4 Block\n197\t- The input is ← + → (step back and strike toward). Startup is 1 frame, followed by **14 guard frames** (TUNE `blockWindow`) and 4 recovery frames. Holding does not extend it; you must input it again.\n198\t- It blocks **high and mid** techniques, including jump kicks. It cannot block **low** (`lowKick`, `frontSweep`, `backSweep`): \"jump them\".\n199\t- Animation: high block (age-uke, forearm up) or middle block (soto-uke, forearm across), picked when the attack's active frames begin.\n200\t- **On a block:** a wood \"clack\", blue sparks and 0.05 s of hit-stop. The attacker is pushed back 14 u and gets **+10 recovery frames**, counting as *mid-technique* (so it is open to a ×2 counter). The defender's guard ends early and it is free 6 frames sooner (frame advantage). The crowd goes \"ooh\". No points.\n201\t\n202\t### 4.5 Scoring a hit\n203\t1. **Quality** `q = 0.5 × timingQ + 0.5 × spacingQ`, so q runs from 0.15 to 1:\n204\t   - `timingQ` is 1.0 if contact happens in the first 40% of the active frames, falling linearly to 0.3 at the last active frame.\n205\t   - `spacingQ` is 1.0 if the contact gap is in the top 30% of the reach band (full extension, *kime*), falling linearly to 0 at the band's minimum (\"jammed\").\n206\t2. `points = lo + Math.round((hi − lo) × q / 100) × 100`, clamped to [lo, hi]. Round Kick and Upper Punch therefore reach 600 only when q ≥ 0.84 (clean). The 500–1000 techniques always reach a full point when they land; their risk is in their long startup and recovery.\n207\t3. **Counter:** if the defender is in a technique's startup, active or recovery frames, or in blocked recoil, then `points = min(1000, points × TUNE.counterMul (2.0))`. Tag the hit \"COUNTER!\".\n208\t4. **Full point** if `points ≥ TUNE.fullAt` (600), worth 1.0 toward winning the round. Otherwise a **half point**, worth 0.5.\n209\t5. Add the points to the scorer's arcade score. These rules are deterministic, with no RNG, which matters for tests and netplay.\n210\t- Examples: a clean Round Kick (q = 1) gives 600, a full point; a sloppy one (q = 0.6) gives 500, a half. A clean Front Kick counter gives 200 × 2 = 400, still a half. A clean Back Kick counter gives 400 × 2 = 800, a full point.\n211\t\n212\t### 4.6 Simultaneous hits (*aiuchi*)\n213\tOn the first valid hit, keep simulating for **6 steps (0.05 s)** before freezing. If the other fighter also lands in that window, the call is **\"AIUCHI! NO POINT\"**. Nobody scores, and both fighters reset.\n214\t\n215\t### 4.7 Hit reactions\n216\t- Half point: the defender staggers back 10 u and holds the hurt pose.\n217\t- Full point or any sweep: the defender is knocked down. Pass `down: 0..1` to the rig, slide back 18 u and lie still. The attacker holds its **kime** pose (the technique's end pose, extended by `hold`) until \"STOP!\".\n218\t- The Bout's own hit-stop is `TUNE.hitstop` (0.10 s). **Do not use `game.freeze`**: the engine's hit-stop skips the scene's `update`, which would stall networking (§12.6). Full points also get 0.35 s of 0.35× slow motion on `game.timeScale` (skipped under reduce motion, and never on an online host: use a visual effect only).\n219\t\n220\t### 4.8 Facing and cross-ups\n221\t- A fighter turns to face its foe when it is **free and grounded**, with a 6-frame pivot animation. During its own technique it never turns.\n222\t- If a foe flips over you while you are mid-technique, you are open from behind, and their hit scores the counter ×2.\n223\t- The `←` (spin) family always strikes **toward the foe** by turning through: Back Kick turns 180° and drives the heel; Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 360°. It is a high-risk, high-value family.\n224\t\n225\t---\n226\t\n227\t## 5. Bout rules and the referee\n228\t\n229\t### 5.1 Terms\n230\t- **Exchange:** from \"BEGIN!\" until \"STOP!\".\n231\t- **Round:** a 30 s clock (`TUNE.clock`); the first to `TUNE.pointsToWin` (2.0) points wins it.\n232\t- **Match:** best of 3 rounds (`roundsToWin` 2) in story and versus. Online and local Kachinuki bouts are a single round.\n233\t\n234\t### 5.2 Bout state machine (`KC.Bout`, deterministic; all timings × `TUNE.refPace`)\n235\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef","uuid":"a9e57b49-d2e2-48e9-b629-f54d88f4f704","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:31:51.158Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174743__x8534bd/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 3. Controls and UX\n\n### 3.1 The twin-stick technique system (the heart of the game)\n- **Left stick = posture** (where your body goes): Neutral, Toward the foe, Away, Up (jump), Down (crouch).\n- **Right stick = technique** (where your strike goes): ↑ high, → (toward the foe) middle, ↓ low, ← (away) the *spinning* family.\n- Directions are **relative to the foe**: \"toward\" is the side the opponent is on, not the screen's right. Fighters face their foe automatically whenever they are free and on the ground.\n- A technique starts on the **press edge** of the technique stick (neutral → a direction), combined with the posture held at that moment. Holding the technique stick does not repeat; it must return to neutral.\n- **Late-posture upgrade window:** if the posture changes from Neutral to a direction within **4 frames (8 steps at 120 Hz)** after a standing technique starts, swap to that posture's variant and keep the elapsed startup frames. This makes \"both sticks at once\" forgiving and adds no latency.\n- **Buffer:** a technique press made while busy (recovery, blockstun) is kept for **0.15 s** (`input.buffered`) and fires on the first free frame.\n- **4-way gates:** both sticks are quantized to 4 directions. Analog sticks use the dominant axis, press at 0.5 and release below 0.35 (hysteresis). For digital posture, vertical beats horizontal (W+D = Up). For the technique keys, the most recent press wins; if two are truly simultaneous, the order is ↑ > ↓ > horizontal.\n\n**The technique matrix** (L posture × R technique). The 15 scoring techniques are in **bold**.\n\n| L posture \\ R technique | R ↑ (high) | R → (toward) | R ↓ (low) | R ← (away / spin) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Neutral** (stand) | **Round Kick** | **Front Kick** (close: **Reverse Punch**) | **Low Kick** | **Back Kick** |\n| **Toward** (step in) | **Upper Lunge Punch** (too close: Round Kick) | **Middle Lunge Punch** (too close: Front Kick) | **Front Foot Sweep** | **Turn Back Kick** |\n| **Away** (step back) | **Back Round Kick** | Block (auto height) | **Back Foot Sweep** | Back Somersault |\n| **Up** (jump) | Front Somersault (flips over the foe) | **Jumping Side Kick** | Jump (straight) | **Jumping Back Kick** |\n| **Down** (crouch) | **Upper Punch** (anti-air) | **Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch** | Crouch | Back Roll |\n\nPosture alone: Neutral = kamae idle, Toward = walk in (72 u/s), Away = walk back (56 u/s), Up = straight jump, Down = crouch while held. With walking counted, that makes about two dozen moves, matching the source's \"24\".\n\n### 3.2 Device mappings\nEngine action names are given in `code`. The game defines its own input preset, `KC_KEYS` (§15.4 EC1 adds the right-stick codes).\n\n| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping, DualSense first) | Keyboard (1 player) | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Posture `up/down/left/right` | Left stick, D-pad | W A S D (arrows also work; the engine fixes them as moves) | — |\n| Technique `tUp/tDown/tLeft/tRight` | **Right stick** (`PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, EC1) **or face buttons**: △ (Pad3) = tUp, ✕ (Pad0) = tDown, □ (Pad2) = tLeft, ○ (Pad1) = tRight | **I K J L** (plus Numpad 8 5/2 4 6) | — |\n| `pause` | Options (Pad9); ignored while Create (Pad8) is held, which is the AI Hub's chord | Esc, P | — |\n| `start` | Options | Enter | — |\n| Menus `confirm` / `cancel` | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right-click; hover moves the focus; the wheel scrolls lists |\n| Dev panel hotkey (only after dev mode is enabled once) | L3+R3 | F2 or Backquote | — |\n\n- **Local 2 players on one keyboard** (`game.addPlayer({ keys: 'right' })`, then `input.use()` with explicit maps; do not rely on `halfKeys`' automatic order): **P1** posture W A S D, technique **T F G H** (T up, G down, F left, H right). **P2** posture arrows, technique **I J K L** and Numpad 8 4 5 6 (5 or 2 = down).\n- Menus accept every joined player's confirm and cancel (the engine's `E.Menu` reads `game._players`).\n- **Face buttons double as a technique stick.** ✕ = confirm in menus, but in a fight there is no confirm; skip prompts use `start` only.\n\n### 3.3 Prompts and glyphs\n- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On a gamepad they show **PlayStation glyphs by default**. Set the engine's standard setting `glyphs` (Controls → Controller → *Button symbols*) to default to `'playstation'` (EC2); players can switch it to **Xbox** (A B X Y). The keyboard shows keycaps and the mouse shows mouse icons.\n- **The 5×7 pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △** (verified: it holds ASCII plus ♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • © × ▸ ♪). Build an icon renderer, `KC.glyph(g, x, y, code, size)`, that draws:\n  - PS badges: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ad8a0`, each on a dark round badge.\n  - Xbox badges: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow.\n  - Stick icons: L or R with a direction arrow, for twin-stick prompts.\n  - Keycaps: a rounded light rectangle with the key label; Space is wide.\n  - Mouse icons with the button highlighted.\n  - Also add ✕ ○ □ △ glyphs to the font (EC3) as a fallback.\n- A technique prompt is two icons, `[L→] + [R↑]` on a pad and `[D] + [I]` on a keyboard. Build it with `KC.promptFor(techId, device)`.\n\n### 3.4 10-foot rules (all screens)\n- Primary menu text uses `scale: 2`. Use no more than 7 items per menu and one primary action per screen. The focus highlight is big and animated (a pulsing bar plus a ▸ cursor). Hold to repeat.\n- A bottom **prompt bar** always shows confirm and back (for example \"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK\", drawn with glyph icons).\n- Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates. The pixel menus use `E.Menu`, which already does this; custom grids (select, stage, records) must implement hover and click with `input.pointer()` and `input.clicked()`.\n- Avoid dense data screens. The records screen shows 10 big rows. The move list is interactive, one posture row at a time (§8.6). The About screen is paged, at most 8 lines per page.\n- Keep titles and pauses clear of the engine's top-right corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`, about 110×60 CSS px).\n\n### 3.5 Touch-only devices\n- Detection: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet. Re-check on every `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`; any keyboard or pad input lifts the gate at once.\n- Set `input.touchEnabled = false` and give every scene `touch: []`, so no touch buttons or stick ever show.\n- The title shows a centred panel: **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, with the engine's corner **settings/menu button** still visible (it opens the standard settings). The attract mode still starts after 10 s and loops. Taps on the canvas only return to the title panel, where the 10 s idle timer restarts the demo.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Combat system\n\n### 4.1 Fighter body and stage geometry\n- The arena walls are at `x0 = 0` and `x1 = 400`. The marks are at **x = 150 (white, left)** and **x = 250 (red, right)**. `y = 0` is the fight plane and `z` is up (side view).\n- Rigs are `new E.Humanoid({ size: 2.2, build: 'heroic', weapon: null, … })`, about 72 px tall at 240 px. The body is an `E.Platformer` on an `E.FightStage(0, 400)` (from the fight module): `w = 8 × size ≈ 18`, `h = 30 × size = 66`. Use the same constructor numbers as `E.Fighter` (`accel 2400, decel 3200, airAccel 0, coyote 0, buffer 0, cut 1`).\n- Movement: walk toward 72 u/s and away 56 u/s. Jump `vz 360` with gravity 1100 (apex ≈ 59 u, ≈ 0.65 s or 39 frames in the air). Front somersault `vz 370`, `vx +140` toward the foe (40 frames in the air, 94 u of travel; it crosses a foe within a gap of ≈ 70). Back somersault `vz 370, vx −126` (85 u). Back roll travels 90 u away in 0.47 s along the ground.\n- **Per-fighter stats and tuning multiply the table:** walk speed × `stats.walk`; reach × `stats.reach` × `TUNE.reachScale`; technique frames ÷ (`stats.speed` × `TUNE.techSpeed`), rounded to whole 120 Hz steps.\n- Push boxes never overlap. Resolve them as `E.Fighter.exchange` does: shared correction, with walls respected.\n- **Hurt boxes come from rig joints** (copy `E.Fighter._boxes()`): head, body and legs. **Hit box:** during active frames, a circle of radius `hitR × size / 2` at the striking joint (`footR`, `handR`, `handL` or `heel`). Crouching lowers the head and body boxes; jumps and flips raise the legs. The **explicit height rules (§4.3) are applied as well**, so results never depend on animation alone.\n\n### 4.2 Technique table (authoritative data; frames are at 60 fps, and the engine steps at 120 Hz, so multiply by 2)\n\"Gap\" means the distance between the two push-box edges at contact. Points go in steps of 100. \"Full\" means the technique can reach a full point (600 or more) without a counter. The source total is the TASVideos figure; ours is about 85% of it, for a slightly snappier modern feel.\n\n| id | Name (JP) | Input | Height | Startup / active / recovery | Total (source) | Travel | Reach (gap) | Points | Full |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `frontKick` | Front Kick (mae-geri) | N + → | mid | 11 / 5 / 28 | 44 (54) | 0 | 6–30 | 100–200 | no |\n| `revPunch` | Reverse Punch (gyaku-zuki) | N + → when gap < 12 | mid | 6 / 4 / 18 | 28 (34) | +4 | 0–14 | 100–200 | no |\n| `roundKick` | Round Kick (mawashi-geri) | N + ↑ | high | 10 / 5 / 22 | 37 (44) | 0 | 6–30 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n| `lowKick` | Low Kick (gedan-geri) | N + ↓ | low, unblockable | 10 / 5 / 28 | 43 (52) | 0 | 4–28 | 100–200 | no |\n| `backKick` | Back Kick (ushiro-geri) | N + ← | mid | 13 / 5 / 23 | 41 (49) | +6 | 10–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `upLunge` | Upper Lunge Punch (jōdan oi-zuki) | → + ↑ (gap < 18: `roundKick`) | high | 15 / 5 / 26 | 46 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `midLunge` | Middle Lunge Punch (chūdan oi-zuki) | → + → (gap < 18: `frontKick`) | mid | 14 / 5 / 26 | 45 (55) | +40 | 18–60 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `frontSweep` | Front Foot Sweep (ashi-barai) | → + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (78) | +10 | 4–34 | 200–400 | counter only |\n| `turnBackKick` | Turn Back Kick | → + ← | mid | 17 / 5 / 26 | 48 (57) | +12 | 12–40 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `backRound` | Back Round Kick (ushiro-mawashi-geri) | ← + ↑ | high | 15 / 5 / 23 | 43 (51) | 0 | 8–34 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `backSweep` | Back Foot Sweep (spinning sweep) | ← + ↓ | low, unblockable, knocks down | 16 / 8 / 36 | 60 (75) | 0 | 6–34 | 100–200 | no |\n| `jumpSide` | Jumping Side Kick (tobi yoko-geri) | ↑ + → | high (air) | 16 / 10 / 26 | 52 (62) | +60 in the air | 30–90 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `jumpBack` | Jumping Back Kick | ↑ + ← | high (air) | 14 / 8 / 22 | 44 (52) | +20 in the air | 8–48 | 500–1000 | yes |\n| `upperPunch` | Upper Punch (age-zuki) | ↓ + ↑ | high, rising (also hits airborne foes in the first half of their arc) | 8 / 7 / 30 | 45 (53) | 0 | 0–18 | 300–600 | yes (only when clean) |\n| `squatPunch` | Squatting Reverse Lunge Punch | ↓ + → | mid-low (also hits crouchers) | 13 / 5 / 30 | 48 (57) | +20 | 10–44 | 400–800 | yes |\n| `block` | Block (uke) | ← + → | auto high or mid | 1 / 14 guard / 4 | 19 (22) | 0 | — | — | — |\n| `frontFlip` | Front Somersault | ↑ + ↑ | evasive | 6 crouch, 40 air, 12 land | 58 (70) | +95 | — | — | — |\n| `backFlip` | Back Somersault | ← + ← | evasive | 6 / 40 / 12 | 58 (70) | −85 | — | — | — |\n| `jump` | Jump | ↑ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 4 / 39 air / 5 | 48 (47) | 0 | — | — | — |\n| `crouch` | Crouch | ↓ (R neutral or ↓) | evasive | 3 in, hold, 5 out | — | — | — | — | — |\n| `backRoll` | Back Roll | ↓ + ← | evasive (low profile, hurt height 24) | 4 / 28 / 8 | 40 (—) | −90 | — | — | — |\n\n**Close substitutions chain:** a Middle Lunge Punch at gap < 12 becomes a Front Kick, and then a Reverse Punch.\n\nStore this as `KC.DATA.techniques` (schema in §15.3). Each entry also carries:\n- `move`: the rig move spec (§10.2);\n- `limb` and `hitR`;\n- `kiai`: `'light'` or `'heavy'`;\n- `bull: true` for the five techniques that work on the bull (`upperPunch, upLunge, squatPunch, frontKick, backKick`, as in the source);\n- `potHeights`: the bonus-stage heights the technique can break.\n\n**The Technique Lab (§14) must measure each technique's real contact gap against a standing dummy, and the tests assert the table within ±4 u.** Tune the rig `reach`/`r1`/`lunge` values until it matches; never change the table to fit the animation.\n\n### 4.3 Height and state rules\n- **High** techniques whiff against a **crouching** foe or one in a **back roll**.\n- **Low** techniques whiff against an **airborne** foe (jump, flips, jump kicks).\n- **Mid** techniques hit standing foes. `squatPunch` also hits crouching foes.\n- `upperPunch` hits standing foes (the head) and **airborne foes up to z 70**. Against an airborne foe it gets +0.25 quality (the anti-air reward).\n- **Somersaults:** while airborne, the tucked body can be hit only by high techniques and `upperPunch`; mid and low pass under it.\n- A fighter that is **down**, **rising**, **frozen** or **returning** cannot be hit.\n\n### 4.4 Block\n- The input is ← + → (step back and strike toward). Startup is 1 frame, followed by **14 guard frames** (TUNE `blockWindow`) and 4 recovery frames. Holding does not extend it; you must input it again.\n- It blocks **high and mid** techniques, including jump kicks. It cannot block **low** (`lowKick`, `frontSweep`, `backSweep`): \"jump them\".\n- Animation: high block (age-uke, forearm up) or middle block (soto-uke, forearm across), picked when the attack's active frames begin.\n- **On a block:** a wood \"clack\", blue sparks and 0.05 s of hit-stop. The attacker is pushed back 14 u and gets **+10 recovery frames**, counting as *mid-technique* (so it is open to a ×2 counter). The defender's guard ends early and it is free 6 frames sooner (frame advantage). The crowd goes \"ooh\". No points.\n\n### 4.5 Scoring a hit\n1. **Quality** `q = 0.5 × timingQ + 0.5 × spacingQ`, so q runs from 0.15 to 1:\n   - `timingQ` is 1.0 if contact happens in the first 40% of the active frames, falling linearly to 0.3 at the last active frame.\n   - `spacingQ` is 1.0 if the contact gap is in the top 30% of the reach band (full extension, *kime*), falling linearly to 0 at the band's minimum (\"jammed\").\n2. `points = lo + Math.round((hi − lo) × q / 100) × 100`, clamped to [lo, hi]. Round Kick and Upper Punch therefore reach 600 only when q ≥ 0.84 (clean). The 500–1000 techniques always reach a full point when they land; their risk is in their long startup and recovery.\n3. **Counter:** if the defender is in a technique's startup, active or recovery frames, or in blocked recoil, then `points = min(1000, points × TUNE.counterMul (2.0))`. Tag the hit \"COUNTER!\".\n4. **Full point** if `points ≥ TUNE.fullAt` (600), worth 1.0 toward winning the round. Otherwise a **half point**, worth 0.5.\n5. Add the points to the scorer's arcade score. These rules are deterministic, with no RNG, which matters for tests and netplay.\n- Examples: a clean Round Kick (q = 1) gives 600, a full point; a sloppy one (q = 0.6) gives 500, a half. A clean Front Kick counter gives 200 × 2 = 400, still a half. A clean Back Kick counter gives 400 × 2 = 800, a full point.\n\n### 4.6 Simultaneous hits (*aiuchi*)\nOn the first valid hit, keep simulating for **6 steps (0.05 s)** before freezing. If the other fighter also lands in that window, the call is **\"AIUCHI! NO POINT\"**. Nobody scores, and both fighters reset.\n\n### 4.7 Hit reactions\n- Half point: the defender staggers back 10 u and holds the hurt pose.\n- Full point or any sweep: the defender is knocked down. Pass `down: 0..1` to the rig, slide back 18 u and lie still. The attacker holds its **kime** pose (the technique's end pose, extended by `hold`) until \"STOP!\".\n- The Bout's own hit-stop is `TUNE.hitstop` (0.10 s). **Do not use `game.freeze`**: the engine's hit-stop skips the scene's `update`, which would stall networking (§12.6). Full points also get 0.35 s of 0.35× slow motion on `game.timeScale` (skipped under reduce motion, and never on an online host: use a visual effect only).\n\n### 4.8 Facing and cross-ups\n- A fighter turns to face its foe when it is **free and grounded**, with a 6-frame pivot animation. During its own technique it never turns.\n- If a foe flips over you while you are mid-technique, you are open from behind, and their hit scores the counter ×2.\n- The `←` (spin) family always strikes **toward the foe** by turning through: Back Kick turns 180° and drives the heel; Turn Back Kick and Back Round Kick turn a full 360°. It is a high-risk, high-value family.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Bout rules and the referee\n\n### 5.1 Terms\n- **Exchange:** from \"BEGIN!\" until \"STOP!\".\n- **Round:** a 30 s clock (`TUNE.clock`); the first to `TUNE.pointsToWin` (2.0) points wins it.\n- **Match:** best of 3 rounds (`roundsToWin` 2) in story and versus. Online and local Kachinuki bouts are a single round.\n\n### 5.2 Bout state machine (`KC.Bout`, deterministic; all timings × `TUNE.refPace`)\n","numLines":160,"startLine":76,"totalLines":1250}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017XFS1pqFusxek1EXMRL4WM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
